  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nSunday will be a down day for shower activity with a brief period\nof shortwave ridging and some slight dry air advection from the \nnorthwest. This will lead to considerably less clouds, virtually \nno rain chances and temperatures climbing back into the upper 70s \nby Monday. Models remain in good agreement that another northern \nPacific waves will continue to be ejected southeastward toward the\nU.S. West Coast starting early next week. Currently models \nsuggest the next potential system reaching the California coast \nearly Monday before reaching our region Monday night into Tuesday,\nwhere additional rain chances will return to the forecast with \nNBM PoPs peaking between 50-80% in south-central AZ and the higher\nterrain areas, however this far out this may need to be adjusted \nbased of how the track of this low progresses. By Wednesday models\nare pointing towards yet again another low system, however larger\ndiscrepancies between models are evident for this system. Due to \nthis confidence is low for rain chances by mid to late next week \nas things are too inconsistent at this time. \n\nDespite if these next systems bring rain to the region or not, a \nmore significant cooling trend can be counted on by the middle of \nthe week as these series of weather systems move in from the \nnorthwest. They will likely usher in a cooler air mass with \nafternoon highs trending more towards the upper 60s to low 70s, \nthe higher or lower end of this range largely being dependent on \nthe depth and position of the systems.",
